#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# from_digits

#% text
# `from_digits(digits)` gives the integer whose decimal digits are `digits`.

#% text
# `from_digits(digits, b)` reads the digits in base `b`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The digits are any integers, also outside `0 .. b - 1`.

#% item
# The base may be a `mixed_radix` list of bases.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
from_digits([2, 0, 2, 6])
#% output
#= 2026

#% cell
from_digits([1, 1, 0, 1], 2)
#% output
#= 13

#% cell
from_digits([15, 15], 16)
#% output
#= 255

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Digits beyond the base are carried:

#% cell
from_digits([1, 12, 3])
#% output
#= 223

#% text
# A mixed radix:

#% cell
from_digits([2, 7, 33, 20], mixed_radix([24, 60, 60]))
#% output
#= 200000

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# `integer_digits` inverts it:

#% cell
integer_digits(from_digits([3, 1, 4, 1, 5], 6), 6)
#% output
#= [3, 1, 4, 1, 5]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `integer_digits`, `mixed_radix`, `integer_reverse`
